\title{Public-Coin Protocols}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-20}

\p{
    An interactive protocol is \strong{public-coin} when every message the verifier sends is a
    uniformly random value, drawn independently of everything that came before, and the verifier
    holds no secrets. The verifier's randomness could be published in advance without harming the
    protocol. Every proof system in this corpus is public-coin, and the property is a hypothesis of
    almost everything built on top of them.
}

\p{
    The term comes from the Arthur–Merlin games of Babai and of Goldwasser and Sipser in the
    mid-1980s, where Arthur's moves are coin flips shown to Merlin. The question at the time was
    whether hiding the verifier's randomness bought any power. It does not: Goldwasser and Sipser
    proved that any interactive proof can be simulated by a public-coin one at the cost of two
    additional messages, so the class of provable statements is the same either way. Restricting
    attention to public-coin protocols is therefore a normalisation rather than a sacrifice, which
    is why the literature took it as the default and why the compilers built later assume it.
}

\p{
    Fix a #{(2\mu+1)}-move protocol between a prover #{P} and a verifier #{V} on common input #{x},
    in which #{P} sends #{a_1, \dots, a_{\mu+1}} and #{V} sends #{c_1, \dots, c_\mu}, alternating.
}

\p{
    \strong{Definition.} The protocol is \em{public-coin} if there are finite non-empty sets
    #{C_1, \dots, C_\mu} — the \em{challenge sets} — such that
    \ul{
        \li{each #{c_i} is distributed uniformly on #{C_i}, independently of #{x} and of the
        [transcript](dhsorens-0054) #{(a_1, c_1, \dots, a_i)} produced so far, and}
        \li{#{V}'s accept-or-reject decision is a deterministic function of #{x} and the full
        transcript.}
    }
}

\p{
    The two clauses do separate work, and both are needed downstream. Independence says a challenge
    carries no information the prover could not already predict the distribution of; this is what
    permits replacing #{c_i} with the output of a hash function without changing what the verifier
    computes, and it is the reason [Fiat–Shamir](dhsorens-002G) applies to these protocols and to no
    others. Determinism of the decision says the verifier keeps no private state, so acceptance is a
    function of the proof alone; this is what makes the compiled proof checkable by a third party
    who watched nothing. Drop the first clause and there is no challenge to derive; drop the second
    and the verifier's verdict is not recoverable from the transcript.
}

\p{
    The contrast is the original interactive proof of Goldwasser, Micali, and Rackoff. In the
    standard protocol for graph non-isomorphism the verifier picks one of the two input graphs at
    random, sends a random isomorphic copy of it, and asks the prover which graph it came from.
    Soundness depends entirely on the prover not knowing which graph was chosen, so the coin cannot
    be public. Nothing can be hashed here: publishing the challenge's derivation publishes the
    answer.
}

\p{
    Three consequences of being public-coin recur throughout this corpus. Fiat–Shamir and the [BCS
    transform](dhsorens-002H) both take a public-coin protocol as input, so it is the entry
    condition for the whole [compilation](dhsorens-004S) pipeline. The soundness notions that
    compilation needs — [round-by-round](dhsorens-004L) and [state-restoration](dhsorens-004M)
    soundness — are defined by quantifying over challenge rounds, which presupposes that the
    challenges are what the definition says they are. And [parallel repetition](dhsorens-004P)
    reduces soundness error exponentially for public-coin arguments, by a theorem of Håstad, Pass,
    Wikström, and Pietrzak, while Bellare, Impagliazzo, and Naor exhibited a four-message argument
    whose error does not decay under repetition at all. The property that fails in their
    counterexample is exactly this one.
}
